course: dictionary
lesson: 6294
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one you need. The word moor is a good example. «A girl lost on a moor in England stayed by a big rock» means a wide, open area of high land with rough grass and heather. «Moor the boat» means to tie the boat up so it stays in one place.
explain: Look at the words around the word. Hills, fog and walking mean the land meaning. Boats, ropes and harbours mean the tying-up meaning. Ask yourself: which meaning fits this sentence? Then try it in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: A capital M is another clue. Moor with a big M is a name for a people, the Moors, who lived in North Africa and ruled much of Spain in the Middle Ages. You will meet this in history books, so read the rest of the sentence carefully.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A moor (noun) is a wide, open area of high land with rough grass, heather and few trees.
fact: To moor (verb) means to tie up a boat or ship so it stays in one place, using ropes or an anchor.
fact: «Moor» with a capital M means one of the Muslim people from North Africa who ruled much of Spain in the Middle Ages.
fact: On moors, the weather can change fast and fog can come down quickly, so take a map and warm clothes. When fog comes in, stop, stay together and follow the path.
exercise: «A girl lost on a moor in England stayed by a big rock.» What is a moor in this sentence? | *A wide, open area of high land with rough grass and heather | A kind of rope | A harbour where boats are tied up | A small town with a church
exercise: «Moor the boat before the tide comes in.» What does moor mean here? | To paint the boat blue | *To tie the boat up so it stays in one place | To sink the boat | To sail the boat very fast
exercise: «When fog comes in on hills or moors, stop, stay together and follow the path.» What should you do when fog comes in? | Walk faster than the others | Take off your warm coat | *Stop, stay together and follow the path | Run ahead to find the edge
test: In «moor the boat», what kind of word is moor? | *A verb | An adjective | A noun | A place name
test: What does Moor with a capital M mean in history? | A king of England | *One of the Muslim people from North Africa who ruled much of Spain in the Middle Ages | A type of boat | A kind of heather
exam: «A girl lost on a moor blew her whistle three times to be heard.» Why did the whistle help? | *It helped rescuers hear her and find her quickly | It told the time | It made the moor warmer | It scared away the sheep
